==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|enquire||chiefly British}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|inquīrō||to seek for}}, composed of {{m|la|in-|t=in, at, on; into}} + {{m|la|quaerō|t=I seek, look for}}, of uncertain origin, but possibly from {{der|en|itc-pro|*kʷaizeō}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kʷeh₂-|t=to acquire}}. Displaced {{cog|enm|enqueren}} (from {{cog|fro|enquerre}}, of the same source) and native {{cog|enm|speir|t=ask, inquire}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈkwaɪɹ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɪnˈkwaɪə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-inquire.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|in|quire}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}} {{tlb|en|ambitransitive}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|US|Canada|Australia}} To [[ask]] (about something).
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=I|text="A fine man, that Dunwody, yonder," commented the young captain, as they parted, and as he turned to his prisoner. "We'll see him on in Washington some day.{{...}}A strong man&mdash;a strong one; and a heedless." ¶ "Of what party is he?" she '''inquired''', as though casually.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make an [[inquiry]] or an [[investigation]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[call]]; to [[name]].
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=X|stanza=12|passage=Canute had his portion from the rest, The which he cald Canutium, for his hyre, Now Cantium, which Kent we commenly '''inquire'''.}}

====Usage notes====
* In British English, the spelling {{m|en|enquire}} is more common, with ''inquire'' often reserved for official [[inquest]]s. In Canada and the US, both spellings are acceptable, though ''inquire'' is favored. In Australian English, ''inquire'' is preferred in all contexts.

